Let $T$ be a rational tree or an interval, let $R:T\times T\to M$ be a box, and put $u(s)=R(s,s)$. There is a coherent family of holomorphic map germs $$c_{s,t}:(M\times M,(u(s),u(t)))\longrightarrow(M,R(s,t))$$ which agrees with ordinary crossing when $s=t$ and satisfies $$\label{rat:differential}
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm d_1c_{s,t}(T_M)&\subset E_1,&\qquad \mathrm d_1c_{s,t}(E_2)&=0,\\
\mathrm d_2c_{s,t}(T_M)&\subset E_2,&\qquad \mathrm d_2c_{s,t}(E_1)&=0.
\end{aligned}$$ Here coherence means that near each parameter pair a single ambient holomorphic map represents all the nearby germs. This family is unique among coherent families agreeing with ordinary crossing on the diagonal.

Let $P\subset M$ be a nonempty connected projective algebraic set. Suppose that $P$ is simply connected and joined by rational chains. There are a connected open neighborhood $U$ of $P$ and a holomorphic map $c:U\times U\to M$ that agrees near the diagonal with ordinary crossing in product charts. Throughout $U\times U$, it satisfies $$\label{cross:identities}
\begin{gathered}
 c(x,x)=x,\qquad
 \mathrm d_1c(T_M)\subset E_1,\quad \mathrm d_1c(E_2)=0,\\
 \mathrm d_2c(T_M)\subset E_2,\quad \mathrm d_2c(E_1)=0.
\end{gathered}$$ The image subbundles in these formulas are evaluated at $c(x,y)$.

For each fiber value $z\in Z$, one can choose a crossing map as above and $r>0$ such that $f^{-1}(B_{\rho}(z,r))\subset U$ and $$\label{global:lipschitz}
 \begin{split}
 d\bigl(c(a,b),c(a',b)\bigr)&\leq d(a,a'),\\
 d\bigl(c(a,b),c(a,b')\bigr)&\leq d(b,b')
 \end{split}$$ whenever all the inputs in the relevant inequality belong to $f^{-1}(B_{\rho}(z,r/10))$.
